Supplementary Table S3
Comparison of the Biological Efficiencies of 3 UV Sources: the Sunshower, the Sun Under 30 Degrees Elevation (Representing a Moderate Sun in Spring in The Netherlands), and the Sun Under 60 Degrees Elevation1
Abbreviations: cUCA, cis-urocanic acid; NMSC, nonmelanoma skin cancer.
Starting point is a situation where a participant receives the same fixed erythemally weighted dose from either of the 3 UV sources. The high sun is taken to be the reference; therefore, all contributions in the table for this UV source are equal to 1. Similarly, as for all UV sources, the erythemally weighted UV dose is assumed to be the same dose, and all elements in the table for erythema are equal to 1. For all other elements, the table gives the amplification factor of the different effect-weighted UV doses from the Sunshower and the low sun when compared with the high sun. The following health effects are considered: erythema, NMSC, immunosuppression, radical formation, cUCA, previtamin D, and previtamin D (5 nm shifted). For example, a person who uses the Sunshower combi creates 90% more free radicals, 45% less cUCA, and 13% more vitamin D (in the 5-nm shifted vitamin D-action spectrum) than in the sun at an elevation of 60 degrees, which is equal to the amplification of this effect for the lower sun (87%).
1
Highest sun in The Netherlands, noon in June.